// THE VOTE MODEL (12 October). Corey: "should receive vote tickets that are tradeable. you should
// be able to redeem them from your inventory if you plan to use them instead of selling them."
//
// So there are two things, and they are deliberately not the same thing:
//
//   VOTE TICKET   a stackable, tradeable obj. What voting hands out. It is an item, so it can be
//                 banked, traded, dropped and lost - and sold to another player, which is the
//                 point: a player who does not want anything in the vote shop can turn a vote into
//                 gp, and a player who does want something can buy more tickets than they voted
//                 for. The ticket is the liquid half.
//
//   VOTE POINT    a varp. What a redeemed ticket becomes, one for one. No slot, no trade, no drop,
//                 no death loss, no way back to a ticket. The bound half.
//
// WHY BOTH, rather than just making the ticket the shop's currency (which the shop system already
// supports - Grace takes Marks of Grace that way, and the TzHaar take Tokkul)? Because then every
// vote reward would be priced in a tradeable item, and the reward shop would be a gp shop with
// extra steps. The one-way redeem is what makes the choice real: tickets or rewards, not both.
//
// REDEEMING IS IRREVERSIBLE, so ~vote_redeem asks before it does a whole stack.

// One ticket, one point. Any other ratio would just be a unit change - the prices are in the same
// currency either way - so 1:1 is the ratio that needs no explaining to a player.
^vote_points_per_ticket = 1

// The stack size past which Redeem asks "one or all?" rather than silently eating the lot. One
// ticket cannot be a mistake; forty can.
^vote_redeem_ask_above = 1

// WHAT THE SHOP CHARGES. The price lives here and is written into the obj's cost= field
// (general/configs/diary_rewards.obj), because that is where the shop system reads a price from -
// ~adjusted_item_cost_buying is oc_cost($obj) scaled by the shopkeeper's per-mille multiplier.
// Grace's recolour kits are priced the same way.
//
// 200 POINTS FOR EXPLORER'S RING 4 (Corey, 23 November): "it's the best item by far because of the
// teleports to everywhere." It was 50, set when the ring was the only thing on the shelf. What the
// ring is worth is not how long it takes to earn, it is that it moves you anywhere: the
// cabbage-patch teleport plus the farm teleport, unlimited, on a ring slot nobody was using.
// Nothing else in the shop changes a day's play that much, so nothing else should cost less than
// it by a nose. 200 is four times the old number and twice the dearest diary piece.
//
// WHAT THAT IS IN DAYS DEPENDS ON LIVE CONFIG THIS FILE CANNOT SEE, and an earlier version of this
// comment guessed at it and was wrong by a factor of ten - it said "a couple of tickets a day",
// and the server pays 10 tickets per 12-hour window (Corey, 23 November). The rate is per-site, in
// data/vote/sites.json on the server: `tickets` and `cooldownHours` per entry, validated by
// engine/src/engine/vote/VoteSites.ts, and it is not in either repository. At 10 a window that is
// 20 points a day if a player takes both windows every day, so 200 is ten days - or twenty at one
// window a day, which is what most people actually do. Weeks, not months.
//
// So price the shelf by what an item DOES, which this file can see, and leave the calendar to
// whoever sets the ticket rate. Change this one number to move the price; nothing else refers to
// 200, and the sim reads it off the obj rather than repeating it.
^vote_price_explorers_ring = 200

// ============================================================================ the elite diary set
// (13 October, Corey: "for the diary items, those will all go in the vote shop - only the ones
// that are actually useful ... that is the only way to receive them", then "do only the level 4
// items, not their lower tiers".)
//
// NINE ITEMS, and the clerk is the only place any of them exists - the achievement diary that
// hands them out in Old School is not implemented here. Which nine and what each one does is in
// general/configs/diary_rewards.constant; these are only the prices.
//
// HOW THEY ARE PRICED. These nine were set in a 40-100 band around Explorer's ring 4 while the ring
// was 50, ranked by how much the item changes a day's play rather than by its stats. The ring has
// since gone to 200 and the band has NOT been rescaled with it, so the nine are now cheap relative
// to the ring by design rather than by arithmetic. The whole set is 600, which at 10 tickets a
// 12-hour window is about a month of voting both windows and two at one a day:
//
//   100  Varrock armour 4        a permanent 10% on every ore and every ore's xp
//    90  Ardougne cloak 4        the best stab cape in the game, +6 prayer, and two teleports
//    80  Falador shield 4        adamant kiteshield defence, +5 prayer, two Prayer restores a day
//    80  Wilderness sword 4      a teleport into deep Wilderness, and webs that always cut
//    60  Karamja gloves 4        the gem mine and a Slayer master, both otherwise a long trip
//    60  Morytania legs 4        rune-platelegs defence and the Ectofuntus run halved
//    50  Desert amulet 4         two teleports, no stats worth wearing it for
//    40  Fremennik sea boots 4   one teleport, and boots nobody is choosing over climbing boots
//    40  Kandarin headgear 4     one teleport, and a helm nobody is choosing over a rune full helm
//
// THE 40s STAY AT 40 (Corey, 23 November): "leave them at 40, they arent that great of items." At
// 10 tickets a 12-hour window that is about two days of voting for a permanent untradeable, which
// looked low against a 200-point ring - but the band is not meant to be even. Fremennik sea boots
// and Kandarin headgear are one teleport each on gear nobody wears for the stats, so the floor is
// where an item that is barely worth buying belongs. Do not raise them to tidy up the spread.
//
// THE SWORD AND THE LEGS WERE THE WRONG WAY ROUND (Corey, 23 November, "swap the sword and legs
// prices") - the sword was 60 and the legs 80. The sword's teleport is the Fountain of Rune, which
// is the hardest useful place on this map to reach any other way, and webs that always cut is a
// fix for crossing the whole Wilderness rather than one convenience. The legs' Ectofuntus half
// halves a chore almost nobody does on this build, and Burgh de Rott can be walked to; the
// rune-platelegs defence is on untradeable legs nobody picks for the stat. Scale-neutral: the set
// still comes to 600, only the order changed.
//
// The whole set is 600 points, which is a long way past any one item - the point of a reward shop
// is that you choose. Each price is one number, used nowhere else; the obj's cost= in
// general/configs/diary_rewards.obj points here.
^vote_price_ardougne_cloak = 90
^vote_price_desert_amulet = 50
^vote_price_falador_shield = 80
^vote_price_fremennik_sea_boots = 40
^vote_price_kandarin_headgear = 40
^vote_price_karamja_gloves = 60
^vote_price_morytania_legs = 60
^vote_price_varrock_armour = 100
^vote_price_wilderness_sword = 80

// ============================================================================ the seed box (23 November)
// THE FIRST THING ON THIS SHELF THAT IS NOT A DIARY REWARD, and it is here for the same reason
// they are: tools/obtainable.py found that nothing in the game gives one. The box has had its fill,
// empty and check handlers in storage_items/scripts/storage_items.rs2 since it was imported - all
// the code to USE one, and no code to produce one. In Old School it is a Tithe Farm reward, and
// there is no Tithe Farm here.
//
// 40 POINTS, WHICH IS THE FLOOR OF THE BAND and not below it. It is worth less to most players
// than anything else on the shelf: it helps one skill, on one kind of trip, and only for somebody
// carrying several sorts of seed at once. But to that player it is used on every run, which is
// more than can be said for the two teleports at 40 - so it matches the floor rather than setting
// a new one underneath it. Nothing else refers to this number.
^vote_price_seed_box = 40

// ============================================================================ claiming (27 October)
// What vote_claim answers with. It cannot answer the real question - "did you vote?" - because that
// is a web request to somebody else's server and a script may not wait for one. So it says what it
// has started doing, and the result arrives as a game message a second or two later.
^vote_claim_started = 0
^vote_claim_nosites = 1
^vote_claim_busy = 2

// The ledger itself would not open - a disk or permissions fault on the server, not anything the
// player did and not anything they can do about it. It is its own answer because the other three
// would all be lies: nothing was started, sites may well be configured, and nobody is busy.
^vote_claim_unavailable = 3

// ============================================================================ the reminder (28 October)
// A player who CAN vote is told so in the chatbox, now and then. A player who has voted is told
// nothing at all - which is the whole point of doing it this way round rather than broadcasting
// every vote to the world: the message only ever goes to somebody it is useful to.
//
// "CAN VOTE" IS READ OFF THE LEDGER, not off a clock. It means at least one configured site whose
// cooldown has run out for this player, so somebody who has never voted qualifies, somebody who
// voted twelve hours ago qualifies again, and somebody who voted ten minutes ago does not. With a
// 24-hour cooldown that is exactly "hasn't voted today"; with a 12-hour one it is better than it.
//
// TWO INTERVALS. The first is short, so a player who logs in and votes nowhere finds out in the
// first few minutes rather than half an hour later; after that it settles into the long one. Both
// are in ticks (100 ticks is a minute) and both are one number used nowhere else.
^vote_reminder_first = 500      // 5 minutes after logging in
^vote_reminder_ticks = 3000     // and every 30 minutes after that

// ============================================================================ where the links are (2 November)
// THE CLERK CANNOT HAND OVER A LINK. This client has no clickable text, so any address it prints
// is one the player has to type by hand - and a vote link only pays if it carries their name,
// because a toplist has no idea who voted unless it was told first. Those two facts do not fit
// together: a link with a name in it is far too long to read off a message box and type.
//
// So the clerk names ONE SHORT ADDRESS and the vote page there puts the player's name into every
// link at once. One thing to type, and it is the thing that pays. Before this the clerk printed
// the sites' own addresses with nobody's name in them, which counted toward the server's ranking
// and could never reach a ticket.
^vote_website = "deathplateau.com/vote"
